---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout 
  title="What is Screen Time and How Does it Affect Our Lives?" 
  description="Definition of screen time, Turkish Ministry of Health child data, sociodemographic risk factors, and the weighted daily screen time calculation formula."
>
  <article class="article-page">
    <div class="container content-width">
      <header class="article-header">
        <a href="/en/bilgi-merkezi" class="back-link">&larr; Knowledge Center</a>
        <span class="article-meta">Guide 01 / Analysis</span>
        <h1>What is Screen Time and How Does it Affect Our Lives?</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">Global digital usage trends, sociodemographic risk factors, and dynamics of screen time calculation.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="article-body">
        <p>
          In the modern information age, digital screens (smartphones, tablets, computers, and televisions) have become central tools occupying the focus of our daily lives. This situation brings along a global phenomenon of <strong>screen dependency</strong>, occupying a vast portion of our waking hours.
        </p>

        <div class="info-box">
          <div class="info-box-title">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
              <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle>
              <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"></line>
              <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"></line>
            </svg>
            Global Average and Waking Time
          </div>
          <p>
            On a global scale, the average daily screen time for an adult internet user ranges between <strong>6 hours 38 minutes and 6 hours 40 minutes</strong>. When an average of 8 hours of sleep is excluded, this data confirms that approximately <strong>42% to 44%</strong> of our waking hours are spent staring at digital screens [1].
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>Screen Time Statistics in Turkey</h2>
        <p>
          In our country, screen exposure has also become a critical developmental and societal issue. According to recent data shared by the Ministry of Health, the <strong>average daily screen time for children aged 6-13 in Turkey has reached a high level of 6.4 hours</strong>. This statistic reveals that school-age children spend almost half of their waking hours in front of screens.
        </p>
        
        <p>
          Similarly, even young children in early childhood (12-36 months) are found to be exposed to screens for an average of 1.2 to 2.2 hours per day, depending on parental attitudes. These high exposures during childhood and adolescence need to be closely monitored due to the risk of affecting developmental processes.
        </p>

        <h2>How is Daily Average Screen Time Calculated?</h2>
        <p>
          To accurately track the general average of your personal screen time, you can utilize the standard weighted formula used in scientific research:
        </p>
        
        <div class="math-box" style="background-color: var(--bg-secondary); padding: var(--space-4); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); text-align: center; font-family: var(--font-heading); font-weight: 500; margin: var(--space-6) 0;">
          {"\\[\\text{Average Daily Screen Time} = \\frac{(\\text{Weekday Time} \\times 5) + (\\text{Weekend Time} \\times 2)}{7}\\]"}
        </div>

        <h2>Sociodemographic Risk Factors</h2>
        <p>
          Research shows that the most significant factors determining screen time are intra-family and socio-economic dynamics:
        </p>
        
        <ul class="bullet-list" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-6); padding-left: var(--space-6); color: var(--text-secondary);">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            <strong>Parental Education and Income Level:</strong> In regions with a low Human Development Index (HDI), the limitation of alternative social, athletic, and cultural recreation areas makes smartphones the cheapest and most accessible entertainment tool, thereby increasing screen time [7].
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            <strong>Parental Role Modeling:</strong> Parents' own screen times are one of the strongest predictors of their children's screen habits.
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            <strong>Parental Attitudes Towards Technology:</strong> As parents' general positive attitudes toward technology use and their tendency to allow children to use technology increase, children's screen time rises significantly.
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            <strong>Household Structure and Sibling Status:</strong> As the number of children and household size increase, children's screen time also increases. Children with siblings have been found to show a tendency to use screens as a tool for peer interaction compared to single children.
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            <strong>Time Management and Intra-Household Dynamics:</strong> Research conducted by Özkılıç Kabul shows that the average daily time mothers spend with their children is 10.02±6.03 hours, whereas this duration remains at 4.58±4.14 hours for fathers. The tendency of mothers to use screens as a temporary \"soother\" or \"assistant caregiver\" due to domestic responsibilities is among the most common factors triggering early-age screen exposure in children.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Inverse Correlation with Development Level</h2>
        <p>
          Conversely, in countries with very high development scores (e.g., Japan, Denmark, Germany), daily screen times are well below the global average. For example, Japan is one of the places with the lowest digital consumption, with an average daily screen time between 3 hours 45 minutes and 4 hours 09 minutes [1]. This demonstrates the importance of strong economic and urban social capital in keeping individuals away from passive screen consumption [7].
        </p>

        <h2>Protect Your Time with Limitra</h2>
        <p>
          Controlling how much time we spend in front of screens is the most fundamental step of digital discipline. <strong>Limitra</strong> can help you protect your pre-determined rational limit decisions and reduce your daily screen time to healthy limits by restricting access to social media and entertainment apps for the rest of the day once your specified duration expires.
        </p>

        <div class="info-box" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
          <div class="info-box-title">A Calmer and More Controlled Screen Time</div>
          <p>
            Limitra is a minimalist Android utility that helps you reclaim hours lost without realizing it and establish digital discipline.
          </p>
          <div style="margin-top: 1rem;">
            <a href="/en/limitra" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">Discover Limitra</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

